1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,200
You press play.

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:04,120
A cassette starts to squeal,
and five minutes later,

3
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:06,380
a game appears on the television.

4
00:00:07,095 --> 00:00:10,615
In an earlier video,
we took that sound apart, bit by bit.

5
00:00:10,915 --> 00:00:13,315
And it ended with a strange claim:

6
00:00:13,549 --> 00:00:17,289
that the world's data centres
still keep their archives on tape.

7
00:00:17,933 --> 00:00:21,133
That sounds like nostalgia. It isn't.

8
00:00:22,321 --> 00:00:26,081
In February 2011,
around forty thousand Gmail users

9
00:00:26,311 --> 00:00:28,901
opened their inbox and found it empty.

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,360
A software update at Google
had a bug in it.

11
00:00:32,903 --> 00:00:37,333
Google keeps several copies of every
mailbox, in different data centres.

12
00:00:37,743 --> 00:00:39,463
The bug reached those too.

13
00:00:40,062 --> 00:00:41,922
Every message was recovered.

14
00:00:42,622 --> 00:00:46,402
It took hours instead of milliseconds,
because it came back from tape.

15
00:00:47,472 --> 00:00:49,112
And Google isn't unusual.

16
00:00:50,032 --> 00:00:52,072
CERN, the particle physics lab,

17
00:00:52,152 --> 00:00:55,412
has stored an exabyte of data
from the Large Hadron Collider.

18
00:00:56,231 --> 00:00:57,931
That's a million terabytes.

19
00:00:58,591 --> 00:01:01,431
Most of it sits
on about sixty thousand tapes.

20
00:01:02,175 --> 00:01:03,675
So here's the question.

21
00:01:04,235 --> 00:01:06,935
We have hard drives, SSDs and the cloud.

22
00:01:07,713 --> 00:01:11,913
Why would the people with the most data
still trust a strip of magnetic tape?

23
00:01:14,553 --> 00:01:17,073
Tape is actually older
than the hard drive.

24
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:23,353
In 1951, the UNIVAC I computer
stored its data on reels of metal tape.

25
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:26,873
The first hard drive
came five years later.

26
00:01:27,581 --> 00:01:30,141
The tape they use today
isn't a music cassette.

27
00:01:30,941 --> 00:01:33,601
It's called LTO,
short for Linear Tape-Open.

28
00:01:34,868 --> 00:01:39,208
HP, IBM and Seagate
developed it in the late 1990s,

29
00:01:39,288 --> 00:01:42,968
as an open format
that anyone could build drives for.

30
00:01:43,433 --> 00:01:47,688
The first cartridge, in 2000,
held one hundred gigabytes.

31
00:01:47,988 --> 00:01:51,828
The newest generation, LTO-10,
holds up to forty terabytes.

32
00:01:52,058 --> 00:01:56,348
Four hundred times as much,
in a box of the same size.

33
00:01:57,246 --> 00:01:59,879
The box often says one hundred.

34
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:03,866
That number assumes your data
can be squeezed two and a half times.

35
00:02:04,146 --> 00:02:08,966
Photos and video are already compressed,
so forty is the honest figure.

36
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:12,410
You can hold it in one hand,
and it holds more than

37
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:16,190
eight hundred million games
for a forty-eight kilobyte Spectrum.

38
00:02:16,791 --> 00:02:19,871
The drive writes four hundred megabytes
every second.

39
00:02:20,551 --> 00:02:23,811
Even so, filling one cartridge
takes more than a day.

40
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:28,060
A ZX Spectrum loaded
about fourteen hundred bits a second.

41
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:31,220
This drive writes more than three billion.

42
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,640
Over two million times faster.

43
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:38,550
How does all of that fit on tape?

44
00:02:39,281 --> 00:02:40,521
First, length.

45
00:02:41,001 --> 00:02:44,361
Inside is a single reel
with more than a kilometre of tape.

46
00:02:44,989 --> 00:02:48,889
It's half an inch wide,
and many times thinner than a human hair.

47
00:02:49,544 --> 00:02:49,944
Then, tracks.

48
00:02:50,174 --> 00:02:53,084
The drive doesn't write
one stripe along the tape.

49
00:02:53,724 --> 00:02:55,224
It writes more than fifteen thousand.

50
00:02:56,469 --> 00:02:59,829
Each one is less than
a thousandth of a millimetre wide.

51
00:03:00,490 --> 00:03:04,370
The head writes thirty-two at once,
runs to the end of the tape,

52
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:07,650
shifts over by a hair's breadth,
and comes back the other way.

53
00:03:08,223 --> 00:03:11,303
Hundreds of passes, back and forth,
over the same kilometre.

54
00:03:12,901 --> 00:03:15,801
Now, the problem. Tape is flexible.

55
00:03:16,421 --> 00:03:19,161
It stretches,
and it wanders sideways as it moves.

56
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:23,910
At these widths, drifting by a single
track means hitting the wrong data.

57
00:03:24,338 --> 00:03:26,798
So the tape carries its own guide rails.

58
00:03:27,278 --> 00:03:30,278
At the factory,
servo patterns are written onto it

59
00:03:30,508 --> 00:03:32,638
before it ever holds a single file.

60
00:03:33,332 --> 00:03:36,092
The drive follows those patterns
the whole time,

61
00:03:36,172 --> 00:03:38,332
steering the head to stay on its track.

62
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:43,297
In LTO-10, the head even tilts slightly,
to make up for the tape

63
00:03:43,527 --> 00:03:46,057
shrinking and swelling
with heat and humidity.

64
00:03:46,772 --> 00:03:48,532
The coating changed too.

65
00:03:49,132 --> 00:03:52,492
Instead of the iron oxide
of old cassettes, modern tape uses tiny,

66
00:03:52,722 --> 00:03:55,752
carefully shaped particles
of barium ferrite.

67
00:03:58,352 --> 00:04:00,092
All of this sounds fragile.

68
00:04:00,772 --> 00:04:03,894
A kilometre of plastic,
racing past a head.

69
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:06,294
But the drive checks itself as it writes.

70
00:04:07,234 --> 00:04:11,794
Just behind the write head sits a read
head, checking every block a moment later.

71
00:04:12,454 --> 00:04:16,254
If it doesn't come back right,
the drive writes it again further along.

72
00:04:16,938 --> 00:04:20,438
On top of that, every block
carries error-correcting codes,

73
00:04:20,738 --> 00:04:24,358
so the drive can rebuild the data
even when part of it is damaged.

74
00:04:25,162 --> 00:04:28,042
Rebuilding data you can't read
sounds impossible.

75
00:04:28,742 --> 00:04:30,722
It deserves a video of its own.

76
00:04:32,932 --> 00:04:38,352
The LTO-9 specification allows one
unreadable bit in every twelve exabytes.

77
00:04:39,577 --> 00:04:43,637
That's about twelve times everything
CERN has stored, for a single error.

78
00:04:45,997 --> 00:04:48,997
But the real advantage
is something tape doesn't do.

79
00:04:49,577 --> 00:04:51,177
It doesn't stay connected.

80
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:54,759
For real protection,
a written cartridge is taken out

81
00:04:54,989 --> 00:04:57,799
of the library altogether
and put on a shelf.

82
00:04:58,543 --> 00:05:00,403
No power. No network.

83
00:05:00,823 --> 00:05:03,923
Nothing that a bug, or an attacker,
can reach through a cable.

84
00:05:04,593 --> 00:05:07,193
That's what saved Gmail in 2011.

85
00:05:07,913 --> 00:05:10,093
The bug reached the copies
that were online.

86
00:05:10,793 --> 00:05:12,113
The tapes weren't.

87
00:05:12,607 --> 00:05:15,767
Today, the same idea
protects against ransomware.

88
00:05:16,547 --> 00:05:19,147
Malware can encrypt every disk
in a building.

89
00:05:19,831 --> 00:05:22,531
It can't encrypt
a cartridge sitting on a shelf.

90
00:05:25,051 --> 00:05:27,671
Some cartridges can be written only once.

91
00:05:28,151 --> 00:05:33,151
After that, nobody can erase them,
not even the person who runs the system.

92
00:05:33,746 --> 00:05:35,946
And sitting on a shelf costs nothing.

93
00:05:36,326 --> 00:05:38,286
Large disk archives often keep

94
00:05:38,516 --> 00:05:42,226
thousands of drives spinning,
using power and making heat.

95
00:05:42,926 --> 00:05:45,746
A cartridge on a shelf
uses no power at all.

96
00:05:46,305 --> 00:05:50,845
Makers rate the cartridges for around
thirty years, if they're stored properly.

97
00:05:51,678 --> 00:05:53,198
Tape is also cheap.

98
00:05:53,898 --> 00:05:57,838
A thirty-terabyte cartridge costs
around two hundred and sixty dollars.

99
00:05:57,918 --> 00:06:00,278
That's under ten dollars a terabyte.

100
00:06:00,843 --> 00:06:04,623
A large new hard drive
costs around forty dollars a terabyte,

101
00:06:04,783 --> 00:06:06,683
more than four times as much.

102
00:06:07,339 --> 00:06:09,199
The catch is the drive itself.

103
00:06:10,139 --> 00:06:13,879
A new LTO-10 drive costs
more than twenty thousand dollars.

104
00:06:13,959 --> 00:06:17,979
So tape pays off when one drive serves
thousands of cartridges.

105
00:06:20,829 --> 00:06:22,989
So why don't we use tape for everything?

106
00:06:23,509 --> 00:06:27,189
Even if we ignore the price,
tape still has one big weakness.

107
00:06:27,967 --> 00:06:30,127
It can't jump straight to your data.

108
00:06:30,887 --> 00:06:34,947
A hard drive moves its head to any file
in a few thousandths of a second.

109
00:06:35,574 --> 00:06:37,034
Tape has to travel.

110
00:06:37,774 --> 00:06:40,634
If your file is at the far end
of a kilometre of tape,

111
00:06:40,714 --> 00:06:43,114
the drive has to spool all the way there.

112
00:06:43,651 --> 00:06:47,951
It's just like rewinding a cassette
to find the right song, or the right game.

113
00:06:48,611 --> 00:06:51,051
Finding a file can take around a minute.

114
00:06:51,671 --> 00:06:54,251
Thousands of times slower
than a hard drive.

115
00:06:54,823 --> 00:06:57,303
That rules tape out
for running a computer,

116
00:06:57,443 --> 00:07:00,203
a database, or millions of tiny files.

117
00:07:00,735 --> 00:07:02,995
But it's perfect for the opposite job.

118
00:07:03,575 --> 00:07:06,995
Huge amounts of data, written once,
and rarely needed again.

119
00:07:09,365 --> 00:07:11,705
The cartridges sit in tape libraries:

120
00:07:11,965 --> 00:07:15,225
cabinets the length of a room,
with thousands of slots inside.

121
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:19,160
A robot runs along a rail,
picks out the right cartridge,

122
00:07:19,390 --> 00:07:21,380
and slides it into a drive.

123
00:07:22,144 --> 00:07:25,024
At Lawrence Livermore,
a laboratory in California,

124
00:07:25,364 --> 00:07:28,064
one library has
nearly twenty thousand slots,

125
00:07:28,344 --> 00:07:30,724
and room for about
three hundred petabytes.

126
00:07:31,392 --> 00:07:33,052
That's three hundred million gigabytes.

127
00:07:34,389 --> 00:07:37,989
To the software, it all looks like
one enormous, rather slow disk.

128
00:07:40,608 --> 00:07:43,208
For decades, that was the whole story.

129
00:07:43,648 --> 00:07:45,868
Tape was the quiet basement
of the data centre.

130
00:07:47,085 --> 00:07:50,665
And then, something changed.
Artificial intelligence.

131
00:07:52,391 --> 00:07:57,491
Training a large AI model takes enormous
collections of text, images and video.

132
00:07:58,119 --> 00:08:00,999
Once it's collected,
that data is worth keeping.

133
00:08:01,759 --> 00:08:03,959
But most of it isn't needed every day.

134
00:08:04,595 --> 00:08:07,655
That's exactly the kind of data
tape was made for.

135
00:08:08,254 --> 00:08:13,034
In October 2026, Fujifilm,
one of the biggest makers of tape,

136
00:08:13,194 --> 00:08:15,754
announced it would spend
about ten billion yen

137
00:08:15,843 --> 00:08:20,143
so it could make roughly five times
as much data tape within three years.

138
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:24,020
The reason it gave?
The spread of generative AI.

139
00:08:25,061 --> 00:08:26,221
The thing we used to wait for

140
00:08:26,451 --> 00:08:28,801
in front of the television
is growing again,

141
00:08:28,881 --> 00:08:31,341
because of the newest technology we have.

142
00:08:32,562 --> 00:08:34,622
And tape still has room to grow.

143
00:08:35,282 --> 00:08:37,702
Its bits are packed roughly
a hundred times less densely

144
00:08:37,932 --> 00:08:39,782
than on the newest hard drives.

145
00:08:40,553 --> 00:08:44,533
In 2020, IBM and Fujifilm
showed a test tape in the lab

146
00:08:44,763 --> 00:08:48,273
that could fit five hundred and eighty
terabytes on a single cartridge.

147
00:08:48,793 --> 00:08:50,633
It isn't a product yet.

148
00:08:50,713 --> 00:08:53,793
But it shows how far tape can go.

149
00:08:56,403 --> 00:08:57,863
There's one catch, though.

150
00:08:57,943 --> 00:08:59,843
And it's a strange one.

151
00:09:00,405 --> 00:09:04,045
The tape lasts for decades.
The drives don't.

152
00:09:04,578 --> 00:09:09,198
For years, a new LTO drive could still
handle tapes two generations old.

153
00:09:09,278 --> 00:09:12,378
From LTO-8, only one.

154
00:09:12,632 --> 00:09:15,832
And an LTO-10 drive
can't read any of them.

155
00:09:16,332 --> 00:09:18,352
Not a single older cartridge.

156
00:09:19,173 --> 00:09:20,013
Why break it?

157
00:09:20,093 --> 00:09:22,073
Remember the guide rails
written at the factory?

158
00:09:22,303 --> 00:09:28,093
LTO-10 changed their pattern, so the head
could follow even thinner tracks.

159
00:09:28,849 --> 00:09:30,969
Older tapes carry the old pattern,

160
00:09:31,049 --> 00:09:33,689
and staying compatible
would have cost capacity.

161
00:09:35,199 --> 00:09:38,259
So what if a drive breaks? Not much.

162
00:09:38,879 --> 00:09:42,419
LTO is an open format,
so any LTO-10 drive,

163
00:09:42,649 --> 00:09:44,979
from any maker, reads the same cartridge.

164
00:09:45,816 --> 00:09:47,936
And the drives are built for hard work.

165
00:09:48,716 --> 00:09:52,916
An LTO-9 drive is rated
for eighty thousand cartridge loads.

166
00:09:53,656 --> 00:09:57,396
In a library, a worn-out drive
is simply swapped for a new one.

167
00:09:57,927 --> 00:09:59,787
Older drives haven't vanished either.

168
00:10:00,017 --> 00:10:05,377
LTO-9 drives are still on sale,
and they read LTO-8.

169
00:10:05,807 --> 00:10:08,007
For anything older, there are used drives,

170
00:10:08,237 --> 00:10:11,147
and companies that rescue
old tapes for a living.

171
00:10:12,301 --> 00:10:14,741
So archives don't just keep their tapes.

172
00:10:15,601 --> 00:10:18,561
Every few years,
they copy everything onto new ones.

173
00:10:19,183 --> 00:10:21,343
So why rate them for thirty years?

174
00:10:22,003 --> 00:10:24,663
Because the tape is never
the reason to move.

175
00:10:25,263 --> 00:10:27,523
Archives copy on their own schedule,

176
00:10:27,603 --> 00:10:31,123
and if a move runs late,
the data is still safe on the shelf.

177
00:10:31,827 --> 00:10:35,207
The tape can outlive
every machine that knows how to play it.

178
00:10:38,317 --> 00:10:40,377
Which brings us back to that cassette.

179
00:10:41,157 --> 00:10:44,257
A strip of tape, a head,
and patterns of magnetism.

180
00:10:44,858 --> 00:10:48,278
More than forty years later,
the idea hasn't changed.

181
00:10:48,878 --> 00:10:51,338
Only the numbers and the technology have.

182
00:10:52,039 --> 00:10:53,239
Somewhere right now,

183
00:10:53,319 --> 00:10:55,579
a robot is pulling a cartridge
off a shelf,

184
00:10:55,659 --> 00:10:58,439
pushing it into a drive,
and pressing play.

185
00:10:59,068 --> 00:11:00,948
So here's a question for you.

186
00:11:01,528 --> 00:11:04,408
If you had a tape drive at home,
what would you put on tape?

187
00:11:04,848 --> 00:11:06,098
Let me know in the comments.
